.TH "btmon" "1" "April 2021" "BlueZ" "Linux System Administration"
.SH Name
btmon \- Bluetooth monitor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.sp
\fBbtmon\fP [\fIOPTIONS\fP ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.sp
The  btmon(1) command  provides  access  to the Bluetooth subsystem monitor
infrastructure for reading HCI traces.
.SH OPTIONS
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.BI \-r \ FILE\fR,\fB \ \-\-read \ FILE
Read traces in btsnoop format from \fIFILE\fP\&.
.TP
.BI \-w \ FILE\fR,\fB \ \-\-write \ FILE
Save traces in btsnoop format to \fIFILE\fP\&.
.TP
.BI \-a \ FILE\fR,\fB \ \-\-analyze \ FILE
Analyze traces in btsnoop format from \fIFILE\fP\&.
It displays the devices found in the \fIFILE\fP with
its packets by type. If gnuplot is installed on
the system it also attempts to plot packet latency
graph.
.TP
.BI \-s \ SOCKET\fR,\fB \ \-\-server \ SOCKET
Start monitor server socket.
.TP
.BI \-p \ PRIORITY\fR,\fB \ \-\-priority \ PRIORITY
Show only priority or lower for user log.
.UNINDENT
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
\fIPRIORITY\fP
T}	T{
NAME
T}
_
T{
\fB3\fP
T}	T{
Error
T}
_
T{
\fB4\fP
T}	T{
Warning
T}
_
T{
\fB6\fP
T}	T{
Information (Default)
T}
_
T{
\fB7\fP
T}	T{
Debug. \fBdebug\fP can be used.
T}
.TE
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.BI \-i \ NUM\fR,\fB \ \-\-index \ NUM
Show only specified controller. \fIhciNUM\fP is also
acceptable. This is useful to capture the traces
from the specific controller when the multiple
controllers are presented.
.TP
.BI \-d \ TTY\fR,\fB \ \-\-tty \ TTY
Read data from \fITTY\fP\&.
.TP
.BI \-B \ SPEED\fR,\fB \ \-\-rate \ SPEED
Set TTY speed. The default \fISPEED\fP is 115300
.TP
.BI \-V \ COMPID\fR,\fB \ \-\-vendor \ COMPID
Set the default company identifier. The \fICOMPID\fP is
a unique number assigned by the Bluetooth SIG to
a member company and can be found/searched from the
Bluetooth SIG webpage.
.sp
For example, Intel is 2 and Realtek is 93.
.TP
.B  \-M\fP,\fB  \-\-mgmt
Open channel for mgmt events.
.TP
.B  \-K\fP,\fB  \-\-kernel
Open kmsg for kernel messages.
.TP
.B  \-t\fP,\fB  \-\-time
Show a time instead of time offset.
.TP
.B  \-T\fP,\fB  \-\-date
Show a time and date information instead of
time offset.
.TP
.B  \-N\fP,\fB  \-\-no\-time
Suppress the time offset display entirely.
.TP
.B  \-S\fP,\fB  \-\-sco
Dump SCO traffic in raw hex format.
.TP
.B  \-A\fP,\fB  \-\-a2dp
Dump A2DP stream traffic in a raw hex format.
.TP
.B  \-I\fP,\fB  \-\-iso
Dump ISO stream traffic in raw hex format. Required
to see LE Audio isochronous data in the output.
.TP
.BI \-E \ IP\fR,\fB \ \-\-ellisys \ IP
Send Ellisys HCI Injection.
.TP
.B  \-P\fP,\fB  \-\-no\-pager
Disable pager usage while reading the log file.
.TP
.BI \-J \ OPTIONS\fR,\fB \ \-\-jlink \ OPTIONS
Read data from RTT.  Each options are comma(,)
separated without spaces.
.UNINDENT
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
\fIOPTIONS\fP
T}	T{
Description
T}
_
T{
\fBDEVICE\fP
T}	T{
Required. Set the target device.
T}
_
T{
\fBSERIALNO\fP
T}	T{
(Optional) Set the USB serial number. Default is \fB0\fP\&.
T}
_
T{
\fBINTERFACE\fP
T}	T{
(Optional) Target interface. Default is \fBswd\fP\&.
T}
_
T{
\fBSPEED\fP
T}	T{
(Optional) Set target interface speed in kHz. Default is \fB1000\fP\&.
T}
.TE
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.BI \-R \ OPTIONS\fR,\fB \ \-\-rtt \ OPTIONS
RTT control block parameters. Each options are
comma(,) separated without spaces.
.UNINDENT
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
\fIOPTIONS\fP
T}	T{
Description
T}
_
T{
\fBADDRESS\fP
T}	T{
(Optional) Address of RTT buffer. Default is \fB0x00\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBAREA\fP
T}	T{
(Optional) Size of range to search in RTT buffer. Default is \fB0\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBNAME\fP
T}	T{
(Optional) Buffer name. Default is \fBbtmonitor\fP
T}
.TE
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.BI \-C \ WIDTH\fR,\fB \ \-\-columns \ WIDTH
Output width if not a terminal
.TP
.BI \-c \ MODE\fR,\fB \ \-\-color \ MODE
Set output color. The possible \fIMODE\fP values are:
\fBauto|always|never\fP\&.
.sp
Default value is \fBauto\fP
.TP
.B  \-v\fP,\fB  \-\-version
Show version
.TP
.B  \-h\fP,\fB  \-\-help
Show help options
.UNINDENT
.SH READING THE OUTPUT
.sp
btmon output is organized as a stream of frames, each representing a single
event in the Bluetooth subsystem. Understanding the output format is essential
for debugging Bluetooth issues.
.SS Line Prefixes
.sp
Every frame begins with a single\-character prefix that identifies its source
and type:
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
T{
Prefix
T}	T{
Meaning
T}	T{
Description
T}
_
T{
\fB<\fP
T}	T{
\fBHCI Command / Data TX\fP
T}	T{
Sent from host to controller (outgoing). HCI commands,
ACL/SCO/ISO data transmitted to the controller.
T}
_
T{
\fB>\fP
T}	T{
\fBHCI Event / Data RX\fP
T}	T{
Received from controller to host (incoming). HCI events,
ACL/SCO/ISO data received from the controller.
T}
_
T{
\fB@\fP
T}	T{
\fBManagement traffic\fP
T}	T{
Management interface (MGMT) commands and events between
bluetoothd and the kernel management layer.
T}
_
T{
\fB=\fP
T}	T{
\fBSystem notes\fP
T}	T{
System\-level annotations: kernel information, index changes,
process log messages, and D\-Bus signals.
T}
.TE
.SS HCI Traffic (\fB<\fP and \fB>\fP)
.sp
HCI frames represent the actual communication between the host software and
the Bluetooth controller hardware.
.sp
\fBAnatomy of an HCI command line\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
< HCI Command: Reset (0x03|0x0003) plen 0             #5 [hci0] 12:35:01.843185
│                    │    │    │          │              │  │       │
│                    │    │    │          │              │  │       └─ Timestamp
│                    │    │    │          │              │  └─ Controller
│                    │    │    │          │              └─ Frame number
│                    │    │    │          └─ Parameter length (bytes)
│                    │    │    └─ Full opcode (16\-bit)
│                    │    └─ OGF|OCF (Opcode Group / Command Field)
│                    └─ Command name (human\-readable)
└─ Direction: < = Host to Controller (outgoing)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAnatomy of an HCI event line\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
> HCI Event: Command Complete (0x0e) plen 4           #6 [hci0] 12:35:01.864922
│                              │      │                │  │       │
│                              │      │                │  │       └─ Timestamp
│                              │      │                │  └─ Controller
│                              │      │                └─ Frame number
│                              │      └─ Parameter length
│                              └─ Event code
│
└─ Direction: > = Controller to Host (incoming)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fB<\fP direction means the host is \fBsending\fP to the controller (commands
and data). The \fB>\fP direction means the controller is \fBsending\fP to the host
(events and data). Think of it from the controller\(aqs perspective: \fB<\fP is
input going into the controller, \fB>\fP is output coming from it.
.sp
\fBHCI commands with parameters\fP are followed by indented detail lines:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
< HCI Command: LE Set Extende.. (0x08|0x0039) plen 2  #1 [hci0] 12:35:01.738352
        Extended advertising: Disabled (0x00)
        Number of sets: Disable all sets (0x00)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBHCI event responses\fP reference the command they complete:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
> HCI Event: Command Complete (0x0e) plen 4           #6 [hci0] 12:35:01.864922
      Reset (0x03|0x0003) ncmd 2
        Status: Success (0x00)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here \fBncmd 2\fP indicates the controller can accept 2 more commands
(HCI flow control). The indented body shows the command this event
completes and the result status.
.sp
\fBLE Meta Events\fP contain a subevent type:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
> HCI Event: LE Meta Event (0x3e) plen 31           #487 [hci0] 12:36:18.974201
      LE Enhanced Connection Complete (0x0a)
        Status: Success (0x00)
        Handle: 2048
        Role: Peripheral (0x01)
        Peer address type: Public (0x00)
        Peer address: AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF (OUI Company)
        Connection interval: 60.00 msec (0x0030)
        Connection latency: 0 (0x0000)
        Supervision timeout: 9600 msec (0x03c0)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBACL Data\fP shows data plane traffic with handle and protocol decoding:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
< LE\-ACL: Handle 2048 [66:B0:26:F1:D3:BC] [1/6] flags 0x00 dlen 16  #493 [hci0] 12:36:18.977915
│   │            │     │                   │          │         │    │    │     │
│   │            │     │                   │          │         │    │    │     └─ Timestamp
│   │            │     │                   │          │         │    │    └─ Controller
│   │            │     │                   │          │         │    └─ Frame number
│   │            │     │                   │          │         └─ Data length
│   │            │     │                   │          └─ flags
│   │            │     │                   └─ Buffer tracking (optional)
│   │            │     └─ Peer address (optional)
│   │            └─ Handle number
│   └─ Connection\-type\-aware label (e.g. BR\-ACL, LE\-ACL, BR\-SCO, LE\-ISO)
└─ Direction marker: \(aq<\(aq = host\->controller (TX), \(aq>\(aq = controller\->host (RX)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
ACL data is automatically decoded into higher\-layer protocols:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
< LE\-ACL: Handle 2048 [2/6] flags 0x00 dlen 7  #494 [hci0] 12:36:18.978488
      ATT: Exchange MTU Request (0x02) len 2
        Client RX MTU: 517

> LE\-ACL: Handle 2048 flags 0x02 dlen 11       #497 [hci0] 12:36:19.000048
      SMP: Pairing Request (0x01) len 6
        IO capability: NoInputNoOutput (0x03)
        OOB data: Authentication data not present (0x00)
        Authentication requirement: Bonding, MITM, SC, No Keypresses, CT2 (0x2d)
        Max encryption key size: 16
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Management Traffic (\fB@\fP)
.sp
Lines starting with \fB@\fP show management interface traffic \-\- the structured
command/event protocol between \fBbluetoothd\fP and the kernel (see
\fBdoc/mgmt\-protocol.rst\fP).
.sp
\fBAnatomy of a management line\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Command: Set Powered (0x0005) plen 1     {0x0001} [hci0] 12:35:04.033564
│                            │        │           │        │       │
│                            │        │           │        │       └─ Timestamp
│                            │        │           │        └─ Controller
│                            │        │           └─ MGMT socket ID
│                            │        └─ Parameter length
│                            └─ MGMT opcode
└─ @ = Management channel
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fB{0x0001}\fP is the management socket identifier \-\- it distinguishes
between multiple management clients (e.g. bluetoothd and btmgmt running
simultaneously).
.sp
\fBMGMT Open/Close\fP track when processes connect to the management channel:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Open: bluetoothd (privileged) version 1.23      {0x0001} 12:34:49.881936
@ MGMT Close: bluetoothd                               {0x0001} 12:35:01.866256
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
These show the process name, privilege level, and protocol version.
.sp
\fBMGMT commands with parameters\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Command: Set Powered (0x0005) plen 1     {0x0001} [hci0] 12:35:04.033564
        Powered: Enabled (0x01)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBMGMT events\fP (responses and notifications):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 7  {0x0001} [hci0] 12:35:04.114789
      Set Powered (0x0005) plen 4
        Status: Success (0x00)
        Current settings: 0x004e0ac1
          Powered
          Secure Simple Pairing
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBMGMT without controller index\fP (global operations):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Command: Read Management Ver.. (0x0001) plen 0  {0x0001} 12:35:04.027771
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 6         {0x0001} 12:35:04.027776
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Notice there is no \fB[hci0]\fP \-\- these operate at the system level,
not on a specific controller.
.SS System Notes (\fB=\fP)
.sp
Lines starting with \fB=\fP are system\-level annotations injected by the
kernel or by processes via the monitor channel. They are \fBnot\fP HCI or
MGMT protocol traffic.
.sp
\fBKernel information\fP (shown at startup):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
= Note: Linux version 6.16.0\-rc6\-0903 (x86_64)                  12:34:49.881926
= Note: Bluetooth subsystem version 2.22                        12:34:49.881930
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBIndex lifecycle\fP (controller added/removed/opened/closed):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
= New Index: 00:11:22:33:44:55 (Primary,USB,hci0)        [hci0] 12:34:49.881932
= Open Index: 00:11:22:33:44:55                          [hci0] 12:34:49.881933
= Index Info: 00:11:22:33:44:55 (OUI Company)              [hci0] 12:34:49.881934
= Close Index: 00:11:22:33:44:55                         [hci0] 12:35:01.865125
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNew Index\fP \-\- a controller was registered with the kernel
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOpen Index\fP \-\- a controller was activated
.IP \(bu 2
\fBIndex Info\fP \-\- controller vendor information
.IP \(bu 2
\fBClose Index\fP \-\- a controller was deactivated
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBProcess log messages\fP (debug output from bluetoothd and other daemons):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
= bluetoothd: src/adapter.c:connected_callback() hci0 devic..   12:36:18.975307
  │           │                                                  │
  │           │                                                  └─ Timestamp
  │           └─ Source file, function, and message (may be truncated)
  └─ Process name
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
These appear when \fBbluetoothd\fP is running with debug enabled (\fB\-d\fP)
or when a process writes to the kernel logging channel. They show the
source file path, function name, and a log message \-\- invaluable for
correlating daemon\-internal decisions with the HCI traffic around them.
.sp
\fBD\-Bus activity\fP (signals and method calls):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
= bluetoothd: [:1.21220:method_call] > org.freedesktop.DBus..   12:34:53.912508
= bluetoothd: [:1.21220:method_return] < [#5]                   12:34:53.912546
= bluetoothd: [signal] org.freedesktop.DBus.ObjectManager.I..   12:36:18.975691
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The format is \fB[bus_name:message_type]\fP followed by \fB>\fP (outgoing) or
\fB<\fP (incoming). Note that \fB>\fP and \fB<\fP within D\-Bus system notes
indicate D\-Bus message direction, not HCI direction.
.SS Right\-Side Metadata
.sp
Every line has metadata right\-aligned at the end. The exact fields depend
on the line type:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
┌─ Main content (left\-aligned, variable width)
│                                        ┌─ Frame # ─┐ ┌Controller┐ ┌─ Timestamp ─┐
│                                        │            │ │          │ │              │
< HCI Command: Reset (0x03|0x0003) plen 0             #5 [hci0] 12:35:01.843185
> HCI Event: Command Complete (0x0e) plen 4           #6 [hci0] 12:35:01.864922
@ MGMT Command: Set Powered (0x0005) plen 1     {0x0001} [hci0] 12:35:04.033564
= Note: Linux version 6.16.0\-rc6\-0903 (x86_64)                  12:34:49.881926
= Open Index: 00:11:22:33:44:55                          [hci0] 12:34:49.881933
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBFrame number\fP (\fB#N\fP): Sequential counter for HCI frames only. Useful
for identifying specific packets in a trace. Only HCI traffic (\fB<\fP and
\fB>\fP) gets frame numbers \-\- MGMT (\fB@\fP) and system notes (\fB=\fP) do not.
.sp
\fBController\fP (\fB[hciN]\fP): Identifies which Bluetooth controller the
frame belongs to. Absent for global operations (kernel notes, MGMT
commands without a controller index).
.sp
\fBMGMT socket ID\fP (\fB{0xNNNN}\fP): Shown on \fB@\fP lines instead of frame
numbers. Identifies which management socket (process) sent the command.
.sp
\fBTimestamp\fP: Always the rightmost field. The format depends on the
command\-line option used:
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
T{
Option
T}	T{
Format
T}	T{
Example
T}
_
T{
\fI(default)\fP
T}	T{
Seconds since trace start
T}	T{
\fB0.881932\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB\-t\fP
T}	T{
Time of day (HH:MM:SS.usec)
T}	T{
\fB12:35:01.843185\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB\-T\fP
T}	T{
Full date and time
T}	T{
\fB2026\-01\-13 12:34:49.881926\fP
T}
.TE
.SS Indented Detail Lines
.sp
Lines indented below a frame header contain the decoded payload of that
frame. The indentation level indicates the protocol layer:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFirst level\fP (6 spaces): direct payload of the HCI/MGMT frame
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSecond level\fP (8 spaces): decoded fields within the payload
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThird level\fP (10+ spaces): nested protocol data (e.g. L2CAP inside
ACL, ATT inside L2CAP)
.UNINDENT
.sp
Example of protocol layering in ACL data:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
> ACL: Handle 2048 flags 0x02 dlen 11       #497 [hci0] 12:36:19.000048
      SMP: Pairing Request (0x01) len 6                          ← L2CAP/SMP layer
        IO capability: NoInputNoOutput (0x03)                    ← SMP fields
        OOB data: Authentication data not present (0x00)
        Authentication requirement: Bonding, MITM, SC (0x2d)
        Max encryption key size: 16
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Timestamp Notes
.sp
When reading btsnoop files with \fB\-t\fP or \fB\-T\fP, timestamps reflect the
wall\-clock time recorded in the btsnoop file. The precision depends on
the source:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLive capture\fP (\fBbtmon\fP monitor channel): Microsecond precision
from the kernel.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbtsnoop files\fP: The btsnoop format stores timestamps as
microseconds since epoch, so full microsecond precision is
preserved. Trailing zeros in the display (e.g., \fB14:38:46.589000\fP)
indicate the original capture source had millisecond granularity.
.UNINDENT
.sp
The default timestamp mode shows seconds elapsed since the first
packet in the trace, which is useful for measuring intervals between
events without needing to know the absolute time.
.SS Frame Numbers vs Line Numbers
.sp
btmon assigns sequential \fBframe numbers\fP (\fB#N\fP) to HCI packets.
These are stable identifiers for specific packets regardless of output
formatting. However, when processing btmon text output with tools like
\fBgrep\fP or \fBsed\fP, the relevant unit is \fBline numbers\fP in the output
file. The two are unrelated:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
A single frame may produce many output lines (header + decoded
fields).
.IP \(bu 2
Frame numbers only apply to HCI traffic (\fB<\fP and \fB>\fP). MGMT
(\fB@\fP) and system notes (\fB=\fP) do not have frame numbers.
.IP \(bu 2
When referencing specific packets, prefer frame numbers (\fB#487\fP)
over line numbers, as frame numbers are stable across different
terminal widths and formatting options.
.UNINDENT
.SS Practical Reading Guide
.sp
\fBTypical command\-response pair\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
< HCI Command: Read BD ADDR (0x04|0x0009) plen 0     #13 [hci0] 12:35:04.057866
> HCI Event: Command Complete (0x0e) plen 10         #14 [hci0] 12:35:04.058750
      Read BD ADDR (0x04|0x0009) ncmd 1
        Status: Success (0x00)
        Address: 00:11:22:33:44:55 (OUI Company)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Read this as: Frame #13, the host asked the controller for its Bluetooth
address. Frame #14, the controller replied with success and the address
\fB00:11:22:33:44:55\fP\&. The response arrived ~0.9ms later.
.sp
\fBTypical MGMT flow showing relation to HCI\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Command: Set Powered (0x0005) plen 1     {0x0001} [hci0] 12:35:04.033564
        Powered: Enabled (0x01)
< HCI Command: Reset (0x03|0x0003) plen 0             #7 [hci0] 12:35:04.033907
> HCI Event: Command Complete (0x0e) plen 4           #8 [hci0] 12:35:04.055753
      Reset (0x03|0x0003) ncmd 2
        Status: Success (0x00)
  ... (more HCI commands to configure the controller) ...
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 7  {0x0001} [hci0] 12:35:04.114789
      Set Powered (0x0005) plen 4
        Status: Success (0x00)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Read this as: bluetoothd sent \fBSet Powered\fP via MGMT. The kernel
translated this into a sequence of HCI commands (Reset, then
configuration). After all HCI commands completed, the kernel sent the
MGMT Command Complete event back to bluetoothd.
.sp
\fBConnection establishment flow\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
> HCI Event: LE Meta Event (0x3e) plen 31           #487 [hci0] 12:36:18.974201
      LE Enhanced Connection Complete (0x0a)
        Status: Success (0x00)
        Handle: 2048
        Role: Peripheral (0x01)
        Peer address: AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF (OUI Company)
@ MGMT Event: Device Connec.. (0x000b) plen 13  {0x0001} [hci0] 12:36:18.974319
= bluetoothd: src/adapter.c:connected_callback() hci0 devic..   12:36:18.975307
< ACL: Handle 2048 [1.. flags 0x00 dlen 16  #493 [hci0] 12:36:18.977915
      LE L2CAP: Connection Parameter Update Request (0x12) ident 1 len 8
< ACL: Handle 2048 [2/6] flags 0x00 dlen 7  #494 [hci0] 12:36:18.978488
      ATT: Exchange MTU Request (0x02) len 2
        Client RX MTU: 517
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Read this as: The controller reported a new LE connection (HCI event).
The kernel forwarded this as a MGMT Device Connected event. bluetoothd
logged its \fBconnected_callback()\fP\&. Then data exchange began \-\- an L2CAP
parameter update and ATT MTU negotiation over the new ACL connection.
.SH ANALYZE MODE
.sp
The \fB\-a\fP (\fB\-\-analyze\fP) option reads a btsnoop file and produces a
statistical summary instead of the full decoded trace.
.SS Usage
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ btmon \-a hcidump.log
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Output Contents
.sp
Analyze mode reports, for each controller found in the trace:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPacket counts\fP: Total HCI packets broken down by type (commands,
events, ACL, SCO, ISO, vendor diagnostics, system notes, user
logs, control messages).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPer\-connection statistics\fP: For each connection handle found:
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
Connection type (BR\-ACL, LE\-ACL, BR\-SCO, BR\-ESCO, LE\-ISO)
.IP \(bu 2
Device address
.IP \(bu 2
TX and RX packet counts and completion counts
.IP \(bu 2
Latency statistics (min, max, median) in milliseconds
.IP \(bu 2
Packet size statistics (min, max, average) in octets
.IP \(bu 2
Throughput estimate in Kb/s
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPer\-channel statistics\fP: For each L2CAP channel within a
connection, the same packet/latency/size statistics.
.IP \(bu 2
.INDENT 2.0
.TP
.B \fBLatency plots\fP: If \fBgnuplot\fP is installed, ASCII\-art latency
distribution plots are rendered in the terminal.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH PROTOCOL ERROR CODES
.sp
btmon automatically decodes error codes from multiple protocol layers.
This section provides a reference for interpreting errors seen across
ATT, SMP, and L2CAP layers.
