.SS LE Credit\-Based Channels
.sp
LE connections use L2CAP signaling on CID 0x0005 for dynamic
channels. The LE Credit Based Connection mechanism provides flow
control:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
< ACL Data TX: Handle 2048 flags 0x00 dlen 18   #600 [hci0] 1.824003
      LE L2CAP: LE Connection Request (0x14) ident 1 len 10
        PSM: 39 (0x0027)
        Source CID: 0x0040
        MTU: 517
        MPS: 251
        Credits: 10

> ACL Data RX: Handle 2048 flags 0x02 dlen 18   #602 [hci0] 1.886556
      LE L2CAP: LE Connection Response (0x15) ident 1 len 10
        Destination CID: 0x0041
        MTU: 517
        MPS: 251
        Credits: 10
        Result: Connection successful (0x0000)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
EATT (Enhanced ATT) uses PSM 0x0027 over LE Credit\-Based channels to
provide multiple parallel ATT bearers.
.SS Connection Parameter Updates
.sp
LE peripherals frequently request connection parameter changes via
L2CAP signaling:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
< ACL Data TX: Handle 2048 flags 0x00 dlen 16   #493 [hci0] 0.003915
      LE L2CAP: Connection Parameter Update Request (0x12) ident 1 len 8
        Min interval: 24
        Max interval: 40
        Peripheral latency: 0
        Timeout multiplier: 256

> ACL Data RX: Handle 2048 flags 0x02 dlen 10   #495 [hci0] 0.066003
      LE L2CAP: Connection Parameter Update Response (0x13) ident 1 len 2
        Result: Connection Parameters accepted (0x0000)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
A result of \fBConnection Parameters rejected (0x0001)\fP means the
central denied the request.
.SS Automating L2CAP Analysis
.sp
\fBFind all L2CAP channel establishments\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqConnection Request\e|Connection Response\e|LE Connection Request\e|LE Connection Response\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBTrack PSM usage\fP (identifies which protocols are active):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqPSM:\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBFind connection parameter update issues\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqParameter Update Request\e|Parameter Update Response\e|Parameters rejected\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBFind EATT channel setup\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqPSM: 39\e|Enhanced Credit\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBTrace a specific L2CAP channel\fP: To follow traffic on a dynamic
channel, note the Source CID and Destination CID from the Connection
Request/Response pair. Then search for those CIDs in subsequent data
frames.
.SS Throughput Estimation
.sp
btmon\(aqs \fB\-\-analyze\fP (\fB\-a\fP) mode computes per\-channel statistics
including throughput.  For each L2CAP channel it reports:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSpeed\fP: Computed as \fBbytes * 8 / latency_sum_ms\fP where
\fBlatency_sum_ms\fP is the sum of inter\-packet deltas (not wall\-clock
duration).  This means idle gaps are excluded, so the figure
represents the \fIactive sending rate\fP rather than application\-level
throughput.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBMin/Avg/Max latency\fP: Per\-packet inter\-arrival time range.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Example output:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
Found TX L2CAP channel with CID 64
      PSM 128 (0x0080)
      Mode: LE Credit
      MTU: 672
      MPS: 490
      TX packets: 29120/29114
      TX Latency: 1\-79 msec (~29 msec)
      TX size: 494\-494 octets (~494 octets)
      TX speed: ~571 Kb/s
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBChannel details shown in analyze mode:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFixed channels\fP (CID <= 7) display their protocol name
(e.g., \fBATT\fP, \fBL2CAP Signaling (LE)\fP).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPSM\fP is shown in both decimal and hexadecimal.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBMode\fP is decoded from Configure Request options (BR/EDR) or
LE signaling (\fBBasic\fP, \fBERTM\fP, \fBLE Credit\fP,
\fBEnhanced Credit\fP, etc.).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBMTU\fP and \fBMPS\fP are extracted from signaling exchanges.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBThroughput caveats:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
The speed value uses inter\-packet latency sums as the denominator,
not wall\-clock elapsed time.  If the sender pauses (e.g., waiting
for credits), the idle period is not counted, which inflates the
reported speed relative to overall throughput.
.IP 2. 3
TX latency is measured from command submission to completion event.
RX latency is the inter\-arrival time between consecutive packets.
These measure different things, so TX and RX speeds for the same
channel are not directly comparable.
.IP 3. 3
For windowed throughput (min/avg/max), btsnoop\-analyzer uses
1\-second sampling windows over wall\-clock time, which provides
a more realistic view of application\-level bandwidth variation.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAutomated throughput extraction\fP from btmon analyze output:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
btmon \-a trace.btsnoop 2>/dev/null | grep \-E \(dqspeed:|Mode:|MTU:|MPS:|PSM\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.\" This file is included by btmon.rst.
.
.SH LE AUDIO PROTOCOL FLOW
.sp
LE Audio uses a multi\-layer protocol stack visible in btmon traces.
The setup sequence involves ATT operations on specific GATT
characteristics (PACS, ASCS) followed by HCI\-level CIS/BIG
management. btmon fully decodes all layers.
.SS PACS: Published Audio Capabilities
.sp
Before audio streaming begins, devices exchange codec capabilities
via the Published Audio Capabilities Service (PACS). The client reads
PACS characteristics to learn what the remote device supports.
.sp
\fBSink PAC read\fP (remote device\(aqs receive capabilities):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
< ACL Data TX: Handle 2048 flags 0x00 dlen 7    #550 [hci0] 0.824003
      ATT: Read Request (0x0a) len 2
        Handle: 0x0075

> ACL Data RX: Handle 2048 flags 0x02 dlen 30   #552 [hci0] 0.886556
      ATT: Read Response (0x0b) len 25
        Handle: 0x0075
          Number of PAC(s): 1
          Codec: LC3 (0x06)
          Codec Specific Capabilities: #0
            Sampling Frequency: 8000 Hz 16000 Hz 24000 Hz 32000 Hz 48000 Hz
            Frame Duration: 7.5 ms 10 ms
            Audio Channel Counts: 1
            Frame Length: 26 \- 240
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The PAC record shows codec capabilities using LTV (Length\-Type\-Value)
encoding. Key fields:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCodec\fP \-\- \fBLC3 (0x06)\fP is the mandatory LE Audio codec
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSampling Frequency\fP \-\- Supported sample rates (bitmask)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFrame Duration\fP \-\- Supported frame durations (7.5 ms and/or 10 ms)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAudio Channel Counts\fP \-\- Supported channel counts
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFrame Length\fP \-\- Min and max octets per codec frame
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAudio Locations\fP (channel assignment):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
> ACL Data RX: Handle 2048 flags 0x02 dlen 9    #554 [hci0] 0.948003
      ATT: Read Response (0x0b) len 4
        Handle: 0x0077
          Location: Front Left
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAvailable Audio Contexts\fP (current use cases):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
> ACL Data RX: Handle 2048 flags 0x02 dlen 9    #558 [hci0] 1.012556
      ATT: Read Response (0x0b) len 4
        Handle: 0x007b
          Sink Context: Media Conversational
          Source Context: Unspecified
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS ASCS: Audio Stream Control
.sp
The Audio Stream Control Service (ASCS) manages the ASE (Audio Stream
Endpoint) state machine. Each ASE transitions through a defined set
of states as streaming is set up and torn down.
.sp
\fBASE State Machine\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
Idle ──► Codec Configured ──► QoS Configured ──► Enabling ──► Streaming
                                                                  │
Idle ◄── Releasing ◄──────── Disabling ◄─────────────────────────┘
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBASE Status notification\fP (state change):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
> ACL Data RX: Handle 2048 flags 0x02 dlen 20   #580 [hci0] 1.456003
      ATT: Handle Value Notification (0x1b) len 15
        Handle: 0x0088
          ASE ID: 0x01
          State: Codec Configured (0x01)
            Framing: Unframed PDUs supported (0x00)
            PHY: 0x02
              LE 2M PHY (0x02)
            RTN: 2
            Max Transport Latency: 10
            Presentation Delay Min: 20000 us
            Presentation Delay Max: 40000 us
            Preferred Presentation Delay Min: 20000 us
            Preferred Presentation Delay Max: 40000 us
            Codec: LC3 (0x06)
              Sampling Frequency: 48000 Hz
              Frame Duration: 10 ms
              Audio Channel Allocation: Front Left
              Frame Length: 120
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBASE Control Point operations\fP drive state transitions. The client
writes to the ASE Control Point characteristic to issue commands:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
< ACL Data TX: Handle 2048 flags 0x00 dlen 25   #582 [hci0] 1.518003
      ATT: Write Request (0x12) len 20
        Handle: 0x008b
          ASE Control Point: Config Codec (0x01)
            ASE ID: 0x01
            Target Latency: Low Latency (0x01)
            PHY: LE 2M PHY
            Codec: LC3 (0x06)
              Sampling Frequency: 48000 Hz
              Frame Duration: 10 ms
              Audio Channel Allocation: Front Left
              Frame Length: 120
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
ASE Control Point commands:
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
T{
Opcode
T}	T{
