.TE
.sp
All characteristics except RAS Features use Client Characteristic
Configuration (CCC) descriptors to enable notifications/indications.
The typical GATT attribute layout uses 18 handles total.
.SS RAS Features
.sp
The RAS Features characteristic is a 4\-byte bitmask read by the
client to discover the server\(aqs capabilities:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
> ACL Data RX: Handle 2048 flags 0x02 dlen 11               #200 [hci0]
      ATT: Read Response (0x0b) len 4
        Handle: 0x0003
          Features: 0x0000000f
            Real\-time Ranging Data (0x00000001)
            Retrieve Lost Ranging Data Segments (0x00000002)
            Abort Operation (0x00000004)
            Filter Ranging Data (0x00000008)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Feature bits:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBBit 0\fP \-\- Real\-time Ranging Data: the server can stream results
via the Real\-time Ranging Data characteristic as CS procedures run.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBBit 1\fP \-\- Retrieve Lost Ranging Data Segments: the client can
request retransmission of missing data segments.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBBit 2\fP \-\- Abort Operation: the client can cancel an in\-progress
data transfer.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBBit 3\fP \-\- Filter Ranging Data: the client can configure which
fields are included in ranging data notifications.
.UNINDENT
.SS RAS Ranging Data Ready
.sp
When a CS procedure completes and results are stored, the server
notifies the client that data is available for retrieval:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
> ACL Data RX: Handle 2048 flags 0x02 dlen 9                #250 [hci0]
      ATT: Handle Value Notification (0x1b) len 2
        Handle: 0x000e
          Counter: 42
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The counter value identifies the specific ranging dataset. The client
uses this counter in subsequent Control Point commands to request or
acknowledge the data.
.SS RAS Ranging Data Overwritten
.sp
If the server\(aqs buffer is full and older results are discarded, this
characteristic notifies the client:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
> ACL Data RX: Handle 2048 flags 0x02 dlen 9                #260 [hci0]
      ATT: Handle Value Notification (0x1b) len 2
        Handle: 0x0011
          Overwritten Count: 38
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This tells the client that ranging data with counter value 38 (and
possibly earlier) has been overwritten and can no longer be retrieved.
.SS RAS Control Point
.sp
The RAS Control Point is the command interface for managing ranging
data transfers. The client writes commands, and the server responds
via indications on the same characteristic.
.sp
\fBOpcodes:\fP
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
T{
Opcode
T}	T{
Command
T}	T{
Description
T}
_
T{
0x00
T}	T{
Get Ranging Data
T}	T{
Request on\-demand transfer of a specific dataset
T}
_
T{
0x01
T}	T{
ACK Ranging Data
T}	T{
Acknowledge receipt of a dataset
T}
_
T{
0x02
T}	T{
Retrieve Lost Ranging Data Segments
T}	T{
Re\-request specific missing segments
T}
_
T{
0x03
T}	T{
Abort Operation
T}	T{
Cancel an in\-progress data transfer
T}
_
T{
0x04
T}	T{
Set Filter
T}	T{
Configure which data fields are included
T}
.TE
.sp
\fBGet Ranging Data\fP \-\- requests transfer of a stored dataset:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
< ACL Data TX: Handle 2048 flags 0x00 dlen 10               #270 [hci0]
      ATT: Write Command (0x52) len 3
        Handle: 0x000b
          Opcode: Get Ranging Data (0x00)
          Ranging Counter: 0x002a
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
After this command, the server sends the data as a sequence of
notifications on the On\-demand Ranging Data characteristic (0x2C16).
.sp
\fBACK Ranging Data\fP \-\- confirms the client received a complete
dataset, allowing the server to free the buffer:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
< ACL Data TX: Handle 2048 flags 0x00 dlen 10               #290 [hci0]
      ATT: Write Command (0x52) len 3
        Handle: 0x000b
          Opcode: ACK Ranging Data (0x01)
          Ranging Counter: 0x002a
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBRetrieve Lost Ranging Data Segments\fP \-\- requests retransmission
of specific segments that were missed or corrupted:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
< ACL Data TX: Handle 2048 flags 0x00 dlen 12               #295 [hci0]
      ATT: Write Command (0x52) len 5
        Handle: 0x000b
          Opcode: Retrieve Lost Ranging Data Segments (0x02)
          Ranging Counter: 0x002a
          First Segment Index: 3
          Last Segment Index: 5
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
A Last Segment Index of \fB0xFF\fP means \(dqall remaining segments from
the first index onward.\(dq
.sp
\fBAbort Operation\fP \-\- cancels any in\-progress data transfer:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
< ACL Data TX: Handle 2048 flags 0x00 dlen 8                #298 [hci0]
      ATT: Write Command (0x52) len 1
        Handle: 0x000b
          Opcode: Abort Operation (0x03)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBSet Filter\fP \-\- configures which fields to include in ranging
data notifications:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
< ACL Data TX: Handle 2048 flags 0x00 dlen 10               #265 [hci0]
      ATT: Write Command (0x52) len 3
        Handle: 0x000b
          Opcode: Set Filter (0x04)
          Filter Configuration: 0x0001
            Mode: 1
            Filter Bit Mask: 0x0000
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The filter configuration is a 16\-bit value: bits [1:0] select the
filter mode, and bits [15:2] provide a field bitmask.
.SS Ranging Data Format
.sp
Both Real\-time (0x2C15) and On\-demand (0x2C16) Ranging Data
characteristics use the same segmented data format. Large datasets
are split across multiple ATT notifications.
.sp
\fBSegmentation Header\fP (1 byte, present in every notification):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
> ACL Data RX: Handle 2048 flags 0x02 dlen 30               #275 [hci0]
      ATT: Handle Value Notification (0x1b) len 25
        Handle: 0x0005
          Segmentation Header: 0x01
            First Segment: True
            Last Segment: False
            Segment Index: 0
          Ranging Data Body:
            Ranging Counter: 0x02a
            Configuration ID: 0
            Selected TX Power: 12 dBm
            Antenna Paths Mask: 0x03
              Antenna Path 1 (0x01)
              Antenna Path 2 (0x02)
            Subevent #0:
              Start ACL Connection Event: 1200
              Frequency Compensation: 150 (0.01 ppm)
              Ranging Done Status: Partial results (0x1)
              Subevent Done Status: All results complete (0x0)
              Ranging Abort Reason: No abort (0x0)
              Subevent Abort Reason: No abort (0x0)
              Reference Power Level: \-20 dBm
              Number of Steps Reported: 8
          Remaining Ranging Data Segment: ...
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Segmentation header fields:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFirst Segment\fP (bit 0) \-\- \fBTrue\fP for the first notification of
a dataset. Only the first segment contains the Ranging Header.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLast Segment\fP (bit 1) \-\- \fBTrue\fP for the final notification.
When both bits are set, the entire dataset fits in one notification.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSegment Index\fP (bits [7:2]) \-\- Sequential index for reassembly.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBRanging Header\fP (4 bytes, only in first segment):
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBRanging Counter\fP (bits [11:0]) \-\- Matches the counter from the
Data Ready notification; identifies the CS procedure instance.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBConfiguration ID\fP (bits [15:12]) \-\- The CS configuration ID
(0\-\-3) used for this measurement.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSelected TX Power\fP (1 byte, signed) \-\- Actual transmit power
used, in dBm.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAntenna Paths Mask\fP (1 byte) \-\- Bitmask indicating which antenna
paths have data: bit 0 = path 1, bit 1 = path 2, etc.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBSubevent Header\fP (per subevent within the ranging data):
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBStart ACL Connection Event\fP \-\- The ACL connection event counter
at which this CS subevent started.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFrequency Compensation\fP \-\- Measured frequency offset in 0.01 ppm
units (signed 16\-bit).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBRanging Done Status\fP \-\- Same codes as HCI subevent results:
\fB0x0\fP = complete, \fB0x1\fP = partial, \fB0xF\fP = aborted.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSubevent Done Status\fP \-\- \fB0x0\fP = complete, \fB0xF\fP = aborted.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBRanging/Subevent Abort Reason\fP \-\- Same abort reason codes as HCI:
\fB0x0\fP = no abort, \fB0x1\fP = host/remote request,
\fB0x2\fP = channel map / no sync, \fB0x3\fP = scheduling,
\fB0xF\fP = unspecified.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBReference Power Level\fP \-\- RSSI reference in dBm (signed).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNumber of Steps Reported\fP \-\- Count of CS step results that follow.
.UNINDENT
.sp
The step data following the subevent header uses the same mode\-specific
format as HCI CS Subevent Result events (Mode 0\-\-3), described in the
CS Step Modes section above.
.sp
Continuation segments contain only the Segmentation Header followed
by raw ranging data bytes that are reassembled with the previous
segments.
.SS RAS Data Transfer State Machine
.sp
The RAS data transfer for a single ranging dataset transitions
through these states:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
┌───────────────┐
│  IDLE         │  No active data transfer.
└─────┬─────────┘
      │ Server: CS procedure completes (HCI level)
      │ Server: notifies Ranging Data Ready (counter=N)
      ▼
┌───────────────┐
│  DATA READY   │  Dataset N is buffered on server.
│  (counter=N)  │  Client has been notified.
└─────┬─────────┘
      │ Client writes: Get Ranging Data (counter=N)
      │ ── OR ── (real\-time mode: automatic push)
      ▼
┌───────────────┐
│  TRANSFERRING  │  Server sends segmented notifications.
│  (counter=N)   │  Segment Index increments: 0, 1, 2, ...
└─────┬──────┬──┘
      │      │ Segment lost? Client writes:
      │      │   Retrieve Lost Segments (counter=N, first, last)
      │      │ Server re\-sends missing segments.
      │      │ (loops back to TRANSFERRING)
      │      │
      │      │ Client writes: Abort Operation
      │      └──────────────────────┐
      │ Last Segment received       │
      ▼                             ▼
┌───────────────┐           ┌───────────────┐
│  COMPLETE     │           │  ABORTED      │
│  (counter=N)  │           └───────┬───────┘
└─────┬─────────┘                   │
      │ Client writes:              │
      │   ACK Ranging Data          │
      │   (counter=N)               │
      ▼                             ▼
┌───────────────┐
│  IDLE         │  Server may free buffer for counter=N.
└───────────────┘
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the server\(aqs buffer fills before the client retrieves data,
a Ranging Data Overwritten notification is sent and the dataset
transitions directly from DATA READY to overwritten (lost).
.SS Typical RAS Data Flow
.sp
A complete RAS session for on\-demand data retrieval:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
1. Client discovers RAS (UUID 0x185B) via GATT primary service discovery
2. Client reads RAS Features (0x2C14) to learn capabilities
3. Client enables CCC notifications on:
     \- RAS Ranging Data Ready (0x2C18)
     \- RAS Ranging Data Overwritten (0x2C19)
     \- RAS On\-demand Ranging Data (0x2C16) or
       RAS Real\-time Ranging Data (0x2C15)
4. CS procedure runs (HCI level)
5. Server notifies Ranging Data Ready with counter=N
6. Client writes Control Point: Get Ranging Data (counter=N)
7. Server sends On\-demand Ranging Data notifications (segmented)
8. Client writes Control Point: ACK Ranging Data (counter=N)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For real\-time streaming, step 6 is skipped \-\- the server pushes
data on the Real\-time Ranging Data characteristic (0x2C15) as each
CS subevent completes, using the same segmented format.
.sp
If segments are lost (e.g., due to ATT MTU limitations or missed
notifications), the client uses Retrieve Lost Ranging Data Segments
to request retransmission of specific segment indices.
.SS Combined HCI + GATT Flow
.sp
The following chart shows the full interleaved timeline of HCI CS
operations and GATT RAS operations as they appear in a btmon trace.
The left column is HCI traffic (\fB< HCI\fP / \fB> HCI\fP), the center
shows GATT ATT traffic (\fB< ACL\fP / \fB> ACL\fP), and the right column
shows the combined state:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
 HCI (CS)                        GATT (RAS)                  State
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

\-\-\- One\-time connection setup \-\-\-

 < LE CS Read Local Supp. Cap.                                IDLE
 > Command Complete                                           │
 < LE CS Read Remote Supp. Cap.                               │
 > Command Status                                             │
 > LE CS Read Remote Supp.                                    │
   Cap. Complete                                              │
 < LE CS Security Enable                                      │
 > Command Status                                             │
 > LE CS Security Enable                                      │
   Complete                                                   │
 < LE CS Set Default Settings                                 │
 > Command Complete                                           ▼
                                                              CAPS EXCHANGED
                                 < ATT: Find By Type Value    │
                                   (RAS UUID 0x185B)          │
                                 > ATT: Find By Type Value    │
                                   Response                   │
                                 < ATT: Read Request           │
                                   (RAS Features 0x2C14)      │
                                 > ATT: Read Response          │
                                   Features: 0x0000000f       │
                                 < ATT: Write Request          │
                                   (CCC: Ready 0x2C18)        │
                                 > ATT: Write Response         │
                                 < ATT: Write Request          │
                                   (CCC: On\-demand 0x2C16)    │
                                 > ATT: Write Response         ▼
                                                              RAS READY

\-\-\- Per\-measurement cycle (repeatable) \-\-\-

 < LE CS Create Config (id=0)                                 │
 > Command Status                                             │
 > LE CS Config Complete                                      ▼
                                                              CONFIGURED (id=0)
 < LE CS Set Proc. Params (id=0)                              │
 > Command Complete                                           ▼
                                                              PARAMS SET (id=0)
 < LE CS Proc. Enable                                         │
   (id=0, enable=1)                                           │
 > Command Status                                             │
 > LE CS Proc. Enable                                         │
   Complete (state=1)                                         ▼
                                                              PROCEDURE RUNNING
 > LE CS Subevent Result  ─┐                                  │
 > LE CS Subevent Result   │ (measurement data                │
   Continue                │  streams from                    │
 > LE CS Subevent Result   │  controller)                     │
 > LE CS Subevent Result  ─┘                                  │
                                                              │
 > LE CS Subevent Result                                      │
   (Procedure Done=0x00)                                      ▼
                                                              PROCEDURE COMPLETE
                                 > ATT: Notification           │
                                   (Data Ready 0x2C18)        │
                                   Counter: N                  ▼
                                                              DATA READY (N)
                                 < ATT: Write Command          │
                                   (Control Point 0x2C17)     │
                                   Get Ranging Data            │
                                   Counter: N                  ▼
                                                              TRANSFERRING (N)
                                 > ATT: Notification           │
                                   (On\-demand 0x2C16)          │
                                   Seg[0]: First=T Last=F     │
                                   Ranging Header + data       │
                                 > ATT: Notification           │
                                   (On\-demand 0x2C16)          │
                                   Seg[1]: First=F Last=F     │
                                 > ATT: Notification           │
                                   (On\-demand 0x2C16)          │
                                   Seg[2]: First=F Last=T     ▼
                                                              TRANSFER COMPLETE (N)
                                 < ATT: Write Command          │
                                   (Control Point 0x2C17)     │
                                   ACK Ranging Data            │
                                   Counter: N                  ▼
                                                              IDLE (buffer freed)

\-\-\- Cleanup (optional) \-\-\-

 < LE CS Remove Config (id=0)
 > Command Status                                             IDLE
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Real\-time Streaming Flow
.sp
When the server supports real\-time ranging data (Features bit 0),
results are pushed immediately as CS subevents complete, without
waiting for a Get Ranging Data request:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
 HCI (CS)                        GATT (RAS)                  State
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

 < LE CS Proc. Enable                                         PARAMS SET
   (id=0, enable=1)
 > LE CS Proc. Enable Complete                                ▼
                                                              PROCEDURE RUNNING
 > LE CS Subevent Result ──┐
                           │     > ATT: Notification
                           │       (Real\-time 0x2C15)
                           │       Seg[0]: First=T Last=T
                           │       Ranging Header +
                           │       subevent data
 > LE CS Subevent Result ──┤
                           │     > ATT: Notification
                           │       (Real\-time 0x2C15)
                           │       Seg[0]: First=T Last=F
                           │     > ATT: Notification
                           │       (Real\-time 0x2C15)
                           │       Seg[1]: First=F Last=T
 > LE CS Subevent Result ──┘
                                 > ATT: Notification
                                   (Real\-time 0x2C15)
                                   ...

 > LE CS Subevent Result                                      │
   (Procedure Done=0x00)                                      ▼
                                                              PROCEDURE COMPLETE
                                 < ATT: Write Command
                                   ACK Ranging Data
                                   Counter: N                  ▼
                                                              IDLE
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In real\-time mode, each HCI Subevent Result triggers one or more
GATT notifications immediately. The Ranging Counter in the data
header increments with each procedure. If a notification segment
is lost, the client can use Retrieve Lost Segments after the
procedure completes (if Features bit 1 is set).
.SS Lost Segment Recovery Flow
.sp
When segments are missed during transfer, the client requests
retransmission:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
 GATT (RAS)                                      State
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 > ATT: Notification (On\-demand 0x2C16)           TRANSFERRING
   Seg[0]: First=T Last=F  ── received
 > ATT: Notification (On\-demand 0x2C16)
   Seg[1]: First=F Last=F  ── received
                                                  │
   Seg[2] ── LOST (not received)                  │
                                                  │
 > ATT: Notification (On\-demand 0x2C16)           │
   Seg[3]: First=F Last=T  ── received            │
                                                  │
 (Client detects gap: Seg[2] missing)             ▼
                                                  INCOMPLETE
 < ATT: Write Command (Control Point 0x2C17)
   Retrieve Lost Ranging Data Segments
   Counter: N
   First Segment Index: 2
   Last Segment Index: 2                          ▼
                                                  RECOVERING
 > ATT: Notification (On\-demand 0x2C16)
   Seg[2]: First=F Last=F  ── re\-sent             ▼
                                                  TRANSFER COMPLETE
 < ATT: Write Command (Control Point 0x2C17)
   ACK Ranging Data
   Counter: N                                     ▼
                                                  IDLE
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS RAS Common Issues
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBFeatures read returns 0x00000000\fP
The server does not support any optional RAS features. Only basic
on\-demand data retrieval via Control Point is available.
.TP
.B \fBRanging Data Ready not received\fP
Check that CCC notifications are enabled on handle 0x2C18. Also
verify the CS procedure actually completed \-\- if the procedure was
aborted, no Data Ready notification is sent.
.TP
.B \fBSegmentation reassembly errors\fP
Verify that Segment Index values are sequential and that the First
Segment flag is set on the initial notification. A missing segment
triggers the Retrieve Lost Segments mechanism if the server supports
it (Features bit 1).
.TP
.B \fBData Overwritten before retrieval\fP
The server has limited buffer space. If the client does not retrieve
and ACK data promptly, older datasets are overwritten. The Data
Overwritten notification indicates the counter value of the lost data.
.TP
.B \fBControl Point write rejected\fP
The connection must be encrypted. RAS Control Point requires write
permission with encryption (\fBWRITE_ENCRYPT\fP). Verify that SMP
pairing has completed.
.UNINDENT
.\" This file is included by btmon.rst.
.
.SH CLASSIC AUDIO PROTOCOL FLOW
.sp
Classic Bluetooth audio uses two main profiles: A2DP for high\-quality
stereo streaming and HFP for voice calls. Both run over BR/EDR ACL
connections and use different transport mechanisms for audio data \-\-
A2DP uses L2CAP\-based AVDTP media channels while HFP uses SCO/eSCO
synchronous connections.
.\" This file is included by btmon-classic-audio.rst.
.
.SS A2DP: Advanced Audio Distribution
.sp
A2DP uses AVDTP (Audio/Video Distribution Transport Protocol) over
L2CAP to negotiate codec parameters and stream high\-quality audio.
btmon fully decodes AVDTP signaling, codec capabilities, and AVRCP
remote control messages.
.SS AVDTP Signaling Channel
.sp
A2DP begins with an L2CAP connection on PSM 25 (0x0019). The first
L2CAP connection on this PSM carries AVDTP signaling; a second
connection on the same PSM carries media transport data:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
< ACL Data TX: Handle 1 flags 0x00 dlen 12
      L2CAP: Connection Request (0x02) ident 1 len 4
        PSM: 25 (0x0019)
        Source CID: 64

> ACL Data RX: Handle 1 flags 0x02 dlen 16
      L2CAP: Connection Response (0x03) ident 1 len 8
        Destination CID: 64
        Source CID: 64
        Result: Connection successful (0x0000)
        Status: No further information available (0x0000)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
After L2CAP configuration completes, AVDTP signaling begins on this
channel.
.SS Stream Endpoint Discovery
.sp
The initiator discovers available Stream Endpoints (SEPs) on the
remote device:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
< ACL Data TX: Handle 1 flags 0x00 dlen 6
      Channel: 64 len 2 [PSM 25 mode Basic (0x00)] {chan 0}
        AVDTP: Discover (0x01) Command (0x00) type 0x00 label 0 nosp 0

> ACL Data RX: Handle 1 flags 0x02 dlen 10
      Channel: 64 len 6 [PSM 25 mode Basic (0x00)] {chan 0}
        AVDTP: Discover (0x01) Response Accept (0x02) type 0x00 label 0 nosp 0
          ACP SEID: 1
            Media Type: Audio (0x00)
            SEP Type: SNK (0x01)
            In use: No
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Each SEP has a SEID (Stream Endpoint Identifier), media type, and
role (Source or Sink). \fBIn use: Yes\fP means the endpoint is already
streaming.
.SS Codec Capability Discovery
.sp
After discovering SEIDs, the initiator queries each endpoint\(aqs
capabilities:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
< ACL Data TX: Handle 1 flags 0x00 dlen 7
      Channel: 64 len 3 [PSM 25 mode Basic (0x00)] {chan 0}
        AVDTP: Get All Capabilities (0x0c) Command (0x00) type 0x00 label 1 nosp 0
          ACP SEID: 1

> ACL Data RX: Handle 1 flags 0x02 dlen 30
      Channel: 64 len 26 [PSM 25 mode Basic (0x00)] {chan 0}
        AVDTP: Get All Capabilities (0x0c) Response Accept (0x02) type 0x00 label 1 nosp 0
          Service Category: Media Transport (0x01)
          Service Category: Media Codec (0x07)
            Media Type: Audio (0x00)
            Media Codec: SBC (0x00)
              Frequency: 0xf0
                16000
                32000
                44100
                48000
              Channel Mode: 0x0f
                Mono
                Dual Channel
                Stereo
                Joint Stereo
              Block Length: 0xf0
                4
                8
                12
                16
              Subbands: 0x0c
                4
                8
              Allocation Method: 0x03
                SNR
                Loudness
              Minimum Bitpool: 2
              Maximum Bitpool: 53
          Service Category: Content Protection (0x04)
            Content Protection Type: SCMS\-T (0x0002)
          Service Category: Delay Reporting (0x08)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Capability responses list all supported values as bitmasks. Key
service categories:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBMedia Transport\fP (0x01) \-\- Always present, indicates the endpoint
supports a media transport channel
.IP \(bu 2
\fBMedia Codec\fP (0x07) \-\- Codec type and supported parameters
.IP \(bu 2
\fBContent Protection\fP (0x04) \-\- Copy protection (SCMS\-T or DTCP)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDelay Reporting\fP (0x08) \-\- Endpoint supports delay reporting
.UNINDENT
.SS Supported Codecs
.sp
btmon decodes several A2DP codecs. Each has a different parameter
format.
.sp
\fBSBC\fP (mandatory A2DP codec):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
Media Codec: SBC (0x00)
  Frequency: 44100 (0x20)
  Channel Mode: Joint Stereo (0x01)
  Block Length: 16 (0x10)
  Subbands: 8 (0x04)
  Allocation Method: Loudness (0x01)
  Minimum Bitpool: 2
  Maximum Bitpool: 53
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAAC\fP (MPEG\-2,4):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
Media Codec: MPEG\-2,4 AAC (0x02)
  Object Type: MPEG\-4 AAC LC (0x40)
  Frequency: 44100 (0x0100)
  Channels: 2 (0x04)
  Bitrate: 256000bps
  VBR: No
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBaptX\fP (Qualcomm, vendor\-specific):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
Media Codec: Non\-A2DP (0xff)
  Vendor ID: Qualcomm Technologies International, Ltd. (APT) (0x0000004f)
  Vendor Specific Codec ID: aptX (0x0001)
    Frequency: 44100 (0x20)
    Channel Mode: Stereo (0x02)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBaptX HD\fP (Qualcomm, vendor\-specific):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
Media Codec: Non\-A2DP (0xff)
  Vendor ID: Qualcomm Technologies, Inc. (0x000000d7)
  Vendor Specific Codec ID: aptX HD (0x0024)
    Frequency: 44100 (0x20)
    Channel Mode: Stereo (0x02)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBLDAC\fP (Sony, vendor\-specific):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
Media Codec: Non\-A2DP (0xff)
  Vendor ID: Sony Corporation (0x0000012d)
  Vendor Specific Codec ID: LDAC (0x00aa)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Vendor codecs appear as \fBNon\-A2DP (0xff)\fP with decoded Vendor ID
and Codec ID. Capability responses list all supported values as
bitmasks; configuration responses show the single selected value.
.SS Stream Configuration
.sp
After selecting a codec and parameters, the initiator sends Set
Configuration:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
< ACL Data TX: Handle 1 flags 0x00 dlen 20
      Channel: 64 len 16 [PSM 25 mode Basic (0x00)] {chan 0}
        AVDTP: Set Configuration (0x03) Command (0x00) type 0x00 label 2 nosp 0
          ACP SEID: 1
          INT SEID: 1
          Service Category: Media Transport (0x01)
          Service Category: Media Codec (0x07)
            Media Type: Audio (0x00)
            Media Codec: SBC (0x00)
              Frequency: 44100 (0x20)
              Channel Mode: Joint Stereo (0x01)
              Block Length: 16 (0x10)
              Subbands: 8 (0x04)
              Allocation Method: Loudness (0x01)
              Minimum Bitpool: 2
              Maximum Bitpool: 53

> ACL Data RX: Handle 1 flags 0x02 dlen 6
      Channel: 64 len 2 [PSM 25 mode Basic (0x00)] {chan 0}
        AVDTP: Set Configuration (0x03) Response Accept (0x02) type 0x00 label 2 nosp 0
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Configuration responses show single selected values instead of
bitmask lists. \fBACP SEID\fP is the remote endpoint, \fBINT SEID\fP is
the local endpoint.
.sp
If configuration is rejected:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
> ACL Data RX: Handle 1 flags 0x02 dlen 8
      Channel: 64 len 4 [PSM 25 mode Basic (0x00)] {chan 0}
        AVDTP: Set Configuration (0x03) Response Reject (0x03) type 0x00 label 2 nosp 0
          Service Category: Media Codec (0x07)
          Error code: Unsupported Configuration (0x29)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Common AVDTP error codes:
