.sp
\fBSynchronous Connection Complete\fP (result):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
> HCI Event: Synchronous Connection Complete (0x2c) plen 17
      Status: Success (0x00)
      Handle: 257
      Address: 11:22:33:44:55:66 (OUI 11\-22\-33)
      Link type: eSCO (0x02)
      Transmission interval: 0x0c
      Retransmission window: 0x06
      RX packet length: 60
      TX packet length: 60
      Air mode: Transparent (0x03)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Key fields:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLink type\fP \-\- \fBSCO (0x00)\fP for legacy, \fBeSCO (0x02)\fP for
enhanced (used by mSBC and LC3\-SWB)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAir mode\fP \-\- \fBCVSD (0x02)\fP for narrow band,
\fBTransparent (0x03)\fP for mSBC or LC3\-SWB
.IP \(bu 2
\fBRX/TX packet length\fP \-\- 60 bytes typical for mSBC T2 settings
.UNINDENT
.SS SCO Data Packets
.sp
After the synchronous connection is established, voice data flows as
SCO/eSCO data packets:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
> BR\-ESCO: Handle 257 flags 0x00 dlen 60
< BR\-ESCO: Handle 257 flags 0x00 dlen 60
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
btmon labels packets based on the connection type established in
Synchronous Connection Complete:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBBR\-SCO\fP \-\- Legacy SCO connection
.IP \(bu 2
\fBBR\-ESCO\fP \-\- Enhanced SCO connection (mSBC, LC3\-SWB, or eSCO CVSD)
.UNINDENT
.sp
SCO data payload is \fBnot displayed by default\fP\&. It requires the
\fB\-\-show\-sco\-data\fP filter to see the hex dump of voice data.
.SS Codec\-Specific Connection Summary
.sp
\fBCVSD (narrow band)\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
HFP codec ID: 1
.IP \(bu 2
HCI codec: CVSD (0x02)
.IP \(bu 2
Voice setting: 0x0060 (Air Coding Format: CVSD)
.IP \(bu 2
Air mode: CVSD (0x02)
.IP \(bu 2
Link type: SCO or eSCO
.IP \(bu 2
Typical packet size: 48 bytes (HV3) or 60 bytes (EV3)
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBmSBC (wide band speech)\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
HFP codec ID: 2
.IP \(bu 2
HCI codec: mSBC (0x05)
.IP \(bu 2
Voice setting: 0x0063 (Air Coding Format: Transparent Data)
.IP \(bu 2
Air mode: Transparent (0x03)
.IP \(bu 2
Link type: eSCO
.IP \(bu 2
Typical packet size: 60 bytes (EV3, T2 settings)
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBLC3\-SWB (super wide band)\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
HFP codec ID: 3
.IP \(bu 2
HCI codec: LC3 (0x06)
.IP \(bu 2
Voice setting: 0x0063 (Air Coding Format: Transparent Data)
.IP \(bu 2
Air mode: Transparent (0x03)
.IP \(bu 2
Link type: eSCO
.IP \(bu 2
Note: btmon displays \fBLC3\fP, not \fBLC3\-SWB\fP
.UNINDENT
.SS Automating HFP Analysis
.sp
\fBIdentify HFP activity\fP \-\- look for RFCOMM on PSM 3:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqPSM: 3\e|RFCOMM:\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBRead AT commands\fP \-\- search hex dump ASCII for AT command patterns:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqAT+B\e|AT+C\e|+BRSF\e|+CIND\e|+CHLD\e|+BCS\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCheck codec negotiation\fP \-\- look for BCS (Bluetooth Codec
Selection):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dq+BCS\e|AT+BAC\e|AT+BCS\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBVerify SCO/eSCO setup\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqSetup Synchronous\e|Enhanced Setup Synchronous\e|Synchronous Connection Complete\e|Write Voice Setting\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCheck voice codec\fP \-\- confirm air mode and coding format:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqAir mode:\e|Air Coding Format:\e|Codec:\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBDetect SCO failures\fP \-\- check Synchronous Connection Complete
status:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqSynchronous Connection Complete\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Then examine the next line for \fBStatus:\fP\&. Common failures:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBConnection Rejected due to Limited Resources (0x0d)\fP \-\- controller
cannot allocate bandwidth
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSCO Offset Rejected (0x2b)\fP \-\- timing parameters rejected
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSCO Interval Rejected (0x2c)\fP \-\- interval parameters rejected
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBTrack call state\fP \-\- look for CIEV indicator updates:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dq+CIEV\e|AT+CHUP\e|ATD\e|ATA\e|AT+CLCC\e|RING\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBFull HFP diagnosis pattern\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 4
Find SDP query for UUID 0x111e/0x111f \-\- confirms HFP discovery
.IP 2. 4
Find L2CAP Connection Request for PSM 3 \-\- RFCOMM channel setup
.IP 3. 4
Find RFCOMM SABM/UA on DLCI 0 then target DLCI \-\- multiplexer and
data channel
.IP 4. 4
Find AT+BRSF in hex dumps \-\- feature exchange, check codec
negotiation bit
.IP 5. 4
Find AT+BAC in hex dumps \-\- available codecs reported
.IP 6. 4
Find +BCS/AT+BCS in hex dumps \-\- codec selected for audio
.IP 7. 4
Find Write Voice Setting \-\- verify air coding format matches codec
.IP 8. 4
Find Setup Synchronous Connection or Enhanced variant \-\- SCO setup
.IP 9. 4
Find Synchronous Connection Complete \-\- check Status, Link type,
Air mode
.IP 10. 4
Find BR\-SCO or BR\-ESCO packets \-\- voice data flowing
.UNINDENT
.\" This file is included by btmon.rst.
.
.SH ADVERTISING AND SCANNING
.sp
btmon decodes advertising data structures automatically. Advertising
and scan response data appears in HCI LE advertising report events
and in advertising command parameters.
.SS Advertising Reports
.sp
When the controller reports received advertisements:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
> HCI Event: LE Meta Event (0x3e) plen 43              #120 [hci0] 0.500003
      LE Extended Advertising Report (0x0d)
        Event type: 0x0013
          Props: 0x0013
            Connectable
            Scannable
            Complete
        Address type: Random (0x01)
        Address: 00:11:22:33:44:55
        Primary PHY: LE 1M
        Secondary PHY: LE 2M
        SID: 0x01
        TX power: 0 dBm
        RSSI: \-55 dBm (0xc9)
        Data length: 18
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The advertising data (AD) structures within the report are decoded
as typed fields:
.sp
\fBCommon AD types btmon decodes\fP:
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
T{
AD Type
T}	T{
Name
T}	T{
Example in btmon output
T}
_
T{
0x01
T}	T{
Flags
T}	T{
\fBFlags: 0x06\fP with decoded bits (LE General Discoverable,
BR/EDR Not Supported)
T}
_
T{
0x02/0x03
T}	T{
Incomplete/Complete 16\-bit UUIDs
T}	T{
\fB16\-bit Service UUIDs (complete): 2 entries\fP
followed by UUID list
T}
_
T{
0x06/0x07
T}	T{
Incomplete/Complete 128\-bit UUIDs
T}	T{
\fB128\-bit Service UUIDs (complete): 1 entry\fP
T}
_
T{
0x08/0x09
T}	T{
Shortened/Complete Local Name
T}	T{
\fBName (complete): MyDevice\fP
T}
_
T{
0x0a
T}	T{
TX Power Level
T}	T{
\fBTX power: 4 dBm\fP
T}
_
T{
0x16
T}	T{
Service Data (16\-bit UUID)
T}	T{
\fBService Data (UUID 0x184e): ...\fP with protocol\-specific
decoding
T}
_
T{
0xff
T}	T{
Manufacturer Specific Data
T}	T{
\fBCompany: Apple, Inc. (76)\fP followed by hex data
T}
.TE
.sp
\fBTypical advertising report\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
> HCI Event: LE Meta Event (0x3e) plen 38              #120 [hci0] 0.500003
      LE Extended Advertising Report (0x0d)
        Address: 00:11:22:33:44:55
        RSSI: \-62 dBm (0xc2)
        Flags: 0x06
          LE General Discoverable Mode
          BR/EDR Not Supported
        Name (complete): LE\-Audio\-Left
        16\-bit Service UUIDs (complete): 3 entries
          Published Audio Capabilities (0x1850)
          Audio Stream Control (0x184e)
          Common Audio (0x1853)
        Service Data (UUID 0x1852): 01a2b3
        Appearance: Earbud (0x0941)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Extended Advertising
.sp
Modern controllers use extended advertising commands and events.
The setup sequence in btmon:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
< HCI Command: LE Set Extended Adv Parameters (0x08|0x0036) plen 25  #50 [hci0] 0.100003
      Handle: 0x01
      Properties: 0x0000
      Min advertising interval: 160.000 msec (0x0100)
      Max advertising interval: 160.000 msec (0x0100)
      Channel map: 37, 38, 39 (0x07)
      Own address type: Random (0x01)
      Peer address type: Public (0x00)
      PHY: LE 1M, LE 2M
      SID: 0x01
      TX power: 7 dBm

< HCI Command: LE Set Extended Adv Data (0x08|0x0037) plen 35  #52 [hci0] 0.101003
      Handle: 0x01
      Operation: Complete extended advertising data (0x01)
      Fragment preference: No fragmentation (0x01)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Periodic Advertising (LE Audio)
.sp
LE Audio broadcast sources use periodic advertising to transmit
BASE announcements containing codec configuration:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
> HCI Event: LE Meta Event (0x3e) plen 80              #200 [hci0] 0.500003
      LE Periodic Advertising Report (0x0f)
        Sync handle: 0x0001
        TX power: 0 dBm
        RSSI: \-45 dBm
        CTE Type: No CTE (0xff)
        Data status: Complete (0x00)
        Data length: 60
        Service Data: Basic Audio Announcement (0x1851)
          Presentation Delay: 40000 us
          Number of Subgroups: 1
            Codec: LC3 (0x06)
              Sampling Frequency: 48000 Hz
              Frame Duration: 10 ms
              Frame Length: 120
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Automating Advertising Analysis
.sp
\fBFind all advertising reports\fP (devices seen):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqAdvertising Report\e|Address:.*RSSI:\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBExtract device names\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqName (complete):\e|Name (short):\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBFind LE Audio devices\fP (by service UUIDs in advertising):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqAudio Stream Control\e|Published Audio Capabilities\e|Common Audio\e|Basic Audio Announcement\e|Broadcast Audio\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBTrack advertising setup\fP (local device configuring advertising):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqSet Extended Adv\e|Set Advertising\e|Set Scan Response\e|Adv Enable\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBFind periodic advertising\fP (broadcast audio):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqPeriodic Advertising\e|PA Sync\e|PA Report\e|Basic Audio Announcement\e|Broadcast.*Announcement\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBIdentify devices by appearance\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqAppearance:\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.\" This file is included by btmon.rst.
.
.SH MGMT PROTOCOL FLOWS
.sp
The Management Interface (MGMT) is the structured command/event protocol
between userspace (typically \fBbluetoothd\fP) and the kernel Bluetooth
subsystem. In btmon output, MGMT traffic is prefixed with \fB@\fP and
provides insight into adapter configuration, device discovery, pairing,
and connection management at a higher level than raw HCI. This section
covers common MGMT protocol flows as seen through btmon.
.sp
For MGMT output format basics (line anatomy, Open/Close, parameters,
events), see the \(dqManagement Traffic\(dq subsection in READING THE OUTPUT
above.
.SS Initialization and Version Handshake
.sp
When \fBbluetoothd\fP starts, it opens a management socket and queries
the kernel for protocol version and supported features. This handshake
must succeed before any controller operations:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Open: bluetoothd (privileged) version 1.23      {0x0001} 12:34:49.881936
@ MGMT Command: Read Management Ver.. (0x0001) plen 0  {0x0001} 12:34:49.882003
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 6         {0x0001} 12:34:49.882010
      Read Management Version Information (0x0001) plen 3
        Status: Success (0x00)
        Version: 1.23
@ MGMT Command: Read Management Sup.. (0x0002) plen 0  {0x0001} 12:34:49.882050
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 58        {0x0001} 12:34:49.882055
      Read Supported Commands (0x0002) plen 55
        Status: Success (0x00)
        Num of commands: 120
        Num of events: 38
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
After the version check, \fBbluetoothd\fP reads the controller list and
queries each controller\(aqs information:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Command: Read Controller Index List (0x0003) plen 0  {0x0001} 12:34:49.882100
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 7              {0x0001} 12:34:49.882105
      Read Controller Index List (0x0003) plen 4
        Status: Success (0x00)
        Num controllers: 1
        Controller: hci0

@ MGMT Command: Read Controller Inf.. (0x0004) plen 0  {0x0001} [hci0] 12:34:49.882200
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 283       {0x0001} [hci0] 12:34:49.882210
      Read Controller Information (0x0004) plen 280
        Status: Success (0x00)
        Address: 00:11:22:33:44:55
        Bluetooth version: 5.4
        Manufacturer: Intel (2)
        Supported settings: 0x003effff
        Current settings: 0x00000080
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Key things to check in the initialization flow:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBMGMT Open\fP \-\- Confirms \fBbluetoothd\fP connected. If missing, the
daemon did not start or crashed before reaching MGMT setup.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBVersion mismatch\fP \-\- If \fBbluetoothd\fP expects a newer MGMT version
than the kernel provides, some features may be unavailable.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNum controllers: 0\fP \-\- No Bluetooth hardware detected. Check
\fBdmesg\fP for driver issues.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCurrent settings\fP \-\- The bitmask shows what is currently enabled on
the controller (Powered, LE, BR/EDR, SSP, etc.).
.UNINDENT
.SS Adapter Configuration
.sp
After reading controller info, \fBbluetoothd\fP configures the adapter
with a series of MGMT commands before powering it on. The exact
sequence depends on \fBmain.conf\fP settings and adapter capabilities.
.sp
\fBLoading stored keys\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Command: Load Link Keys (0x0012) plen 3  {0x0001} [hci0] 12:34:50.001200
      Debug keys: Disabled (0x00)
      Key count: 0
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 4   {0x0001} [hci0] 12:34:50.001220
      Load Link Keys (0x0012) plen 1
        Status: Success (0x00)

@ MGMT Command: Load Long Term Keys (0x0013) plen 2  {0x0001} [hci0] 12:34:50.001300
      Key count: 0
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 4        {0x0001} [hci0] 12:34:50.001315
      Load Long Term Keys (0x0013) plen 1
        Status: Success (0x00)

@ MGMT Command: Load Identity Resolving Keys (0x0030) plen 2  {0x0001} [hci0] 12:34:50.001400
      Key count: 0
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 4                {0x0001} [hci0] 12:34:50.001415
      Load Identity Resolving Keys (0x0030) plen 1
        Status: Success (0x00)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
When bonded devices exist, the Key count will be non\-zero and btmon
will list each key entry. A large number of keys can indicate many
paired devices; this is normal.
.sp
\fBSetting adapter properties\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Command: Set Secure Connections (0x002d) plen 1  {0x0001} [hci0] 12:34:50.002100
      Secure connections: Enabled (0x01)
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 7          {0x0001} [hci0] 12:34:50.002120
      Set Secure Connections (0x002d) plen 4
        Status: Success (0x00)
        Current settings: 0x004e0a81

@ MGMT Command: Set Bondable (0x0009) plen 1            {0x0001} [hci0] 12:34:50.002200
      Bondable: Enabled (0x01)
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 7          {0x0001} [hci0] 12:34:50.002220
      Set Bondable (0x0009) plen 4
        Status: Success (0x00)
        Current settings: 0x004e0a91
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBPowering on\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Command: Set Powered (0x0005) plen 1     {0x0001} [hci0] 12:35:04.033564
      Powered: Enabled (0x01)
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 7   {0x0001} [hci0] 12:35:04.114789
      Set Powered (0x0005) plen 4
        Status: Success (0x00)
        Current settings: 0x004e0ac1
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Between the \fBSet Powered\fP command and its response, btmon will show
the HCI commands the kernel sends to initialize the radio (see
HCI INITIALIZATION SEQUENCE).
.SS Discovery
.sp
Device discovery is initiated through MGMT rather than raw HCI commands.
\fBbluetoothd\fP (or \fBbtmgmt\fP) sends a Start Discovery command
specifying which transport types to scan.
.sp
\fBStarting discovery\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Command: Start Discovery (0x0023) plen 1  {0x0001} [hci0] 12:36:00.100200
      Address type: 0x07
        BR/EDR
        LE Public
        LE Random
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 5   {0x0001} [hci0] 12:36:00.100500
      Start Discovery (0x0023) plen 2
        Status: Success (0x00)
        Address type: 0x07
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
After this, btmon shows the HCI\-level scan commands the kernel issues
(\fBLE Set Scan Parameters\fP, \fBLE Set Scan Enable\fP, and/or
\fBInquiry\fP for BR/EDR). Discovered devices appear as MGMT events:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Event: Device Found (0x0012) plen 38      {0x0001} [hci0] 12:36:00.250003
      LE Address: AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF (Random)
      RSSI: \-62
      Flags: 0x0000
      EIR Data:
        Name (complete): My Device
        TX power: 0
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBService discovery\fP uses a filtered variant:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Command: Start Service Discovery (0x003a) plen 19  {0x0001} [hci0] 12:36:10.100200
      Address type: 0x06
        LE Public
        LE Random
      RSSI threshold: \-127
      UUIDs: 1
        UUID: Heart Rate (0x180d)
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 5            {0x0001} [hci0] 12:36:10.100500
      Start Service Discovery (0x003a) plen 2
        Status: Success (0x00)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBStopping discovery\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Command: Stop Discovery (0x0024) plen 1   {0x0001} [hci0] 12:36:15.200100
      Address type: 0x07
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 5    {0x0001} [hci0] 12:36:15.200400
      Stop Discovery (0x0024) plen 2
        Status: Success (0x00)

@ MGMT Event: Discovering (0x0013) plen 2         {0x0001} [hci0] 12:36:15.200500
      Address type: 0x07
      Discovery: Disabled (0x00)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Discovery problems to look for:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBStatus: Busy (0x0a)\fP on Start Discovery \-\- Another discovery
session is already active.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBStatus: Not Powered (0x0f)\fP \-\- The adapter is not powered on.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBStatus: RFKilled (0x12)\fP \-\- The radio is blocked by rfkill.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNo Device Found events\fP \-\- The remote device may not be
advertising, or address type filtering may be wrong.
.UNINDENT
.SS Pairing and Bonding via MGMT
.sp
MGMT provides a high\-level pairing interface. The host sends a
\fBPair Device\fP command; the kernel coordinates SMP or SSP as
appropriate and reports the result.
.sp
\fBInitiating pairing\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Command: Pair Device (0x0019) plen 8      {0x0001} [hci0] 12:37:00.500200
      LE Address: AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF (Random)
      Capability: KeyboardDisplay (0x04)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
After this command, btmon shows the underlying SMP or SSP exchange
(see SMP PAIRING FLOW and HCI INITIALIZATION SEQUENCE for
details). User interaction events may appear:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Event: User Confirmation Request (0x000f) plen 12  {0x0001} [hci0] 12:37:01.200100
      LE Address: AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF (Random)
      Value: 123456

@ MGMT Command: User Confirmation Reply (0x001e) plen 6   {0x0001} [hci0] 12:37:03.800200
      LE Address: AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF (Random)
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 10            {0x0001} [hci0] 12:37:03.800350
      User Confirmation Reply (0x001e) plen 7
        Status: Success (0x00)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Or for passkey entry:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Event: User Passkey Request (0x0010) plen 6        {0x0001} [hci0] 12:37:01.200100
      LE Address: AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF (Random)

@ MGMT Command: User Passkey Reply (0x0020) plen 10       {0x0001} [hci0] 12:37:05.100200
      LE Address: AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF (Random)
      Passkey: 123456
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBPairing success\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Event: New Long Term Key (0x000a) plen 37   {0x0001} [hci0] 12:37:06.100200
      Store hint: Yes (0x01)
      LE Address: AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF (Random)
      Key type: Authenticated P\-256 (0x03)
      Central: 0x00
      Encryption size: 16

@ MGMT Event: New Identity Resolving Key (0x0018) plen 30  {0x0001} [hci0] 12:37:06.100300
      Store hint: Yes (0x01)
      Random address: AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF
      LE Address: 11:22:33:44:55:66 (Public)
      Key: 00112233445566778899aabbccddeeff

@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 10   {0x0001} [hci0] 12:37:06.100500
      Pair Device (0x0019) plen 7
        Status: Success (0x00)
        LE Address: AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF (Random)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Key events to check after pairing:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNew Long Term Key\fP with \fBStore hint: Yes\fP \-\- The kernel is
telling userspace to persist this key for future reconnections.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNew Identity Resolving Key\fP \-\- Reveals the device\(aqs real address
behind an RPA. This is essential for address resolution on reconnect.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBKey type\fP \-\- \fBAuthenticated P\-256\fP indicates Secure Connections
with MITM protection. \fBUnauthenticated P\-256\fP means Just Works SC.
\fBAuthenticated\fP (no P\-256) means Legacy with MITM.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBPairing failure\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 10   {0x0001} [hci0] 12:37:06.100500
      Pair Device (0x0019) plen 7
        Status: Authentication Failed (0x05)
        LE Address: AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF (Random)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
MGMT pairing error codes:
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
T{
Code
T}	T{
Status
T}	T{
Diagnostic Meaning
T}
_
T{
0x03
T}	T{
Failed
T}	T{
Generic failure; check SMP or HCI errors preceding this
T}
_
T{
0x04
T}	T{
Connect Failed
T}	T{
Could not establish connection before pairing
T}
_
T{
0x05
T}	T{
Authentication Failed
T}	T{
SMP or SSP authentication did not succeed; check SMP Pairing
Failed reason or HCI Authentication Failure event
T}
_
T{
0x08
T}	T{
Timeout
T}	T{
Pairing timed out; remote device may have gone out of range
T}
_
T{
0x0b
T}	T{
Rejected
T}	T{
Remote device rejected the pairing request
T}
_
T{
0x0d
T}	T{
Invalid Parameters
T}	T{
Bad address type or capability value in the Pair Device command
T}
.TE
.SS Device Connection and Disconnection
.sp
MGMT reports connection lifecycle events that complement the HCI\-level
events shown elsewhere in the trace.
.sp
\fBDevice connected\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Event: Device Connected (0x000b) plen 13   {0x0001} [hci0] 12:36:18.974319
      LE Address: AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF (Random)
      Flags: 0x0000
      EIR Data Length: 0
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This event follows the HCI \fBLE (Enhanced) Connection Complete\fP event.
It tells userspace that a new connection is ready.
.sp
\fBDevice disconnected\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Event: Device Disconnected (0x000c) plen 8  {0x0001} [hci0] 12:38:20.500200
      LE Address: AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF (Random)
      Reason: Connection timeout (0x01)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Disconnect reasons reported by MGMT:
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
T{
Code
T}	T{
Reason
T}	T{
Diagnostic Meaning
T}
_
T{
0x00
T}	T{
Unspecified
T}	T{
No reason provided; typically a local disconnect
T}
_
T{
0x01
T}	T{
Connection timeout
T}	T{
Link supervision timeout expired; device may be out of range
T}
_
T{
0x02
T}	T{
Connection terminated by local host
T}	T{
Local side initiated the disconnection
T}
_
T{
0x03
T}	T{
Connection terminated by remote host
T}	T{
Remote device initiated the disconnection
T}
.TE
.sp
\fBUnpair Device\fP removes stored keys and optionally disconnects:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Command: Unpair Device (0x001a) plen 7     {0x0001} [hci0] 12:39:00.100200
      LE Address: AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF (Random)
      Disconnect: Enabled (0x01)
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 10    {0x0001} [hci0] 12:39:00.100500
      Unpair Device (0x001a) plen 7
        Status: Success (0x00)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Advertising via MGMT
.sp
Modern BlueZ configures advertising through MGMT commands rather than
sending HCI LE advertising commands directly. This provides a
multi\-client advertising infrastructure.
.sp
\fBAdding an advertisement\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Command: Add Advertising (0x003e) plen 22   {0x0001} [hci0] 12:40:00.100200
      Instance: 1
      Flags: 0x0006
        The connectable flag will be managed
        The limited discoverable flag will be managed
      Duration: 0
      Timeout: 0
      Advertising data length: 6
      Scan response length: 0
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 5      {0x0001} [hci0] 12:40:00.100500
      Add Advertising (0x003e) plen 2
        Status: Success (0x00)
        Instance: 1
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBRemoving an advertisement\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Command: Remove Advertising (0x003f) plen 1  {0x0001} [hci0] 12:41:00.100200
      Instance: 1
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 5       {0x0001} [hci0] 12:41:00.100500
      Remove Advertising (0x003f) plen 2
        Status: Success (0x00)
        Instance: 1
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For the HCI\-level advertising commands that result from these MGMT
operations, see ADVERTISING AND SCANNING\&.
.SS Error Diagnosis
.sp
MGMT Command Status and Command Complete events carry error codes that
are distinct from HCI error codes. When diagnosing failures, check
both layers.
.sp
\fBMGMT error in Command Complete\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Event: Command Complete (0x0001) plen 4    {0x0001} [hci0] 12:42:00.100200
      Set Powered (0x0005) plen 1
        Status: RFKilled (0x12)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBMGMT Command Status\fP (asynchronous command accepted or rejected):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@ MGMT Event: Command Status (0x0002) plen 3     {0x0001} [hci0] 12:42:01.100200
      Start Discovery (0x0023) plen 0
        Status: Busy (0x0a)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
