.TE
.sp
\fBCorrelating MGMT and HCI errors\fP: When MGMT reports \fBFailed\fP
(0x03) or \fBAuthentication Failed\fP (0x05), look at the HCI events
immediately preceding the MGMT response. The HCI\-level error
(e.g., \fBAuthentication Failure (0x05)\fP, \fBConnection Timeout
(0x08)\fP) provides the specific hardware\-level cause.
.SS Automating MGMT Analysis
.sp
\fBList all MGMT commands and events\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dq@ MGMT\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBFind MGMT errors (non\-zero status)\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dq@ MGMT\(dq output.txt | grep \-v \(dqStatus: Success\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBTrack adapter power state changes\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqSet Powered\e|RFKilled\e|Not Powered\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBFind all discovery activity\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqStart Discovery\e|Stop Discovery\e|Device Found\e|Discovering\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBFind pairing activity via MGMT\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqPair Device\e|User Confirmation\e|User Passkey\e|New Long Term Key\e|New Identity Resolving Key\e|Authentication Failed\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBFind connection lifecycle events\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqDevice Connected\e|Device Disconnected\e|Unpair Device\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBIdentify which MGMT clients are active\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dq@ MGMT Open\e|@ MGMT Close\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBFull MGMT diagnosis pattern\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Find \fBMGMT Open\fP \-\- confirm \fBbluetoothd\fP connected and note
socket ID
.IP 2. 3
Check \fBRead Controller Information\fP \-\- verify address, version,
and current settings
.IP 3. 3
Find \fBSet Powered\fP \-\- confirm the adapter was powered on
successfully
.IP 4. 3
Search for non\-Success status codes \-\- these indicate failures
.IP 5. 3
For pairing issues, find \fBPair Device\fP and trace forward to its
\fBCommand Complete\fP response
.IP 6. 3
For connection issues, find \fBDevice Connected\fP/\fBDevice
Disconnected\fP and check the disconnect reason
.UNINDENT
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Capture the traces from hci0 to hcidump.log file
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ btmon \-i hci0 \-w hcidump.log
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Open the trace file
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ btmon \-r hcidump.log
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Open the trace file with wall\-clock timestamps
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ btmon \-t \-r hcidump.log
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Open the trace file with full date and time
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ btmon \-T \-r hcidump.log
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH AUTOMATED TRACE ANALYSIS
.sp
This section provides guidance for analyzing btmon traces
programmatically or with AI assistance. Each topic references
the detailed protocol section earlier in this document.
.SS Recommended Workflow
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 4
\fBGet an overview\fP: Start with \fBbtmon \-a <file>\fP to see packet
counts, connection handles, device addresses, and traffic volumes.
.IP 2. 4
\fBDecode with timestamps\fP: Use \fBbtmon \-t \-r <file> > output.txt\fP
to produce a text file with wall\-clock timestamps for analysis.
.IP 3. 4
\fBIdentify connections\fP: Search for connection establishment events
to build a handle\-to\-address mapping:
.INDENT 4.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqConnection Complete\e|Enhanced Connection Complete\e|CIS Established\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 4. 4
\fBTrack disconnections\fP: Search for disconnect events and their
reasons:
.INDENT 4.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqDisconnect Complete\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Then examine the lines following each match for the \fBReason:\fP
field. See HCI ERROR AND DISCONNECT REASON CODES for
interpretation.
.IP 5. 4
\fBCheck pairing/security\fP: Search for SMP activity (see
SMP PAIRING FLOW):
.INDENT 4.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqPairing Request\e|Pairing Response\e|Pairing Failed\e|Encryption Change\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 6. 4
\fBIdentify LE Audio\fP: Search for ASCS and CIS activity (see
LE AUDIO PROTOCOL FLOW):
.INDENT 4.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqASE Control Point\e|CIG Parameters\e|Create Connected Isochronous\e|CIS Established\e|Setup ISO Data Path\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 7. 4
\fBCheck for errors\fP: Search across all protocol layers (see
PROTOCOL ERROR CODES):
.INDENT 4.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# HCI\-level errors
grep \-n \(dqStatus:\(dq output.txt | grep \-v \(dqSuccess\(dq

# ATT\-level errors
grep \-n \(dqError Response\(dq output.txt

# SMP failures
grep \-n \(dqPairing Failed\(dq output.txt

# L2CAP rejections
grep \-n \(dqConnection refused\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 8. 4
\fBExtract GATT discovery\fP: Filter the GATT service/characteristic
discovery traffic (see RECONSTRUCTING A GATT DATABASE FROM SNOOP
TRACES):
.INDENT 4.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# Find all service discovery responses
grep \-n \(dqRead By Group Type Response\e|Attribute group list\e|Handle range.*UUID\(dq output.txt

# Find all characteristic discovery responses
grep \-n \(dqRead By Type Response\e|Properties:\e|Value Handle:\e|Value UUID:\(dq output.txt

# Find all descriptor discovery responses
grep \-n \(dqFind Information Response\e|Format:\e|Handle:.*UUID:\(dq output.txt

# Find targeted service searches
grep \-n \(dqFind By Type Value\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 9. 4
\fBCheck L2CAP channels\fP: Identify protocol usage and channel
issues (see L2CAP CHANNEL TRACKING):
.INDENT 4.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqPSM:\e|Connection Request\e|Connection Response\e|Parameter Update\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 10. 4
\fBCheck advertising\fP: See what devices are visible and what
they advertise (see ADVERTISING AND SCANNING):
.INDENT 4.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
grep \-n \(dqAdvertising Report\e|Name (complete):\e|Appearance:\(dq output.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Key Patterns for Connection Lifecycle
.sp
A complete connection lifecycle for an LE ACL connection follows this
pattern in the trace:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
\fBLE Enhanced Connection Complete\fP \-\- connection established,
note the Handle and Peer address
.IP 2. 3
\fBLE Connection Update Complete\fP \-\- connection parameters changed
(may occur zero or more times)
.IP 3. 3
\fBEncryption Change\fP \-\- link encrypted (may show encryption
algorithm). See SMP PAIRING FLOW for the SMP exchange that
precedes this.
.IP 4. 3
ACL Data with ATT/SMP/L2CAP \-\- service discovery and data exchange.
See RECONSTRUCTING A GATT DATABASE FROM SNOOP TRACES for GATT,
L2CAP CHANNEL TRACKING for channel setup, and
PROTOCOL ERROR CODES for error interpretation.
.IP 5. 3
\fBDisconnect Complete\fP \-\- connection ended, check Reason field.
See HCI ERROR AND DISCONNECT REASON CODES for reason codes.
.UNINDENT
.sp
For LE Audio connections, additional steps appear between 3 and 5
(see LE AUDIO PROTOCOL FLOW for full details):
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
ATT operations on PACS/ASCS characteristics (codec negotiation)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLE Set CIG Parameters\fP command and response
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLE Create CIS\fP command
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLE CIS Established\fP event (note the CIS handle)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLE Setup ISO Data Path\fP command
.IP \(bu 2
ISO Data TX/RX (audio streaming)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDisconnect Complete\fP on CIS handle (stream ended)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLE Remove CIG\fP (group removed)
.UNINDENT
.SS Common Debugging Scenarios
.sp
\fBPairing failure diagnosis\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Find \fBPairing Request\fP \-\- note IO capabilities and auth
requirements
.IP 2. 3
Find \fBPairing Response\fP \-\- compare to determine association model
.IP 3. 3
If \fBPairing Failed\fP appears, the reason code identifies the
failure (see SMP PAIRING FLOW)
.IP 4. 3
If \fBEncryption Change\fP shows \fBStatus: Success\fP, pairing
succeeded
.IP 5. 3
If no SMP traffic on reconnect but \fBEncryption Change\fP fails,
the bond was lost on one side
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBAudio streaming failure diagnosis\fP (see LE AUDIO PROTOCOL FLOW):
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Check PACS reads \-\- do both devices support compatible codecs?
.IP 2. 3
Check ASE Control Point Config Codec \-\- was it accepted?
.IP 3. 3
Check ASE state notifications \-\- did the ASE reach Streaming?
.IP 4. 3
Check \fBCIS Established\fP \-\- was Status Success?
.IP 5. 3
Check \fBSetup ISO Data Path\fP \-\- was it configured?
.IP 6. 3
Check for ISO Data packets \-\- is audio actually flowing?
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBGATT operation rejected\fP (see PROTOCOL ERROR CODES):
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Find \fBError Response\fP \-\- note the error code
.IP 2. 3
\fBInsufficient Encryption\fP (0x0f) → expect \fBLE Start Encryption\fP
to follow
.IP 3. 3
\fBAuthentication Insufficient\fP (0x05) → expect SMP pairing to
follow
.IP 4. 3
After security is established, the ATT operation should be retried
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBConnection parameter negotiation failure\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Find \fBConnection Parameter Update Request\fP (L2CAP level)
.IP 2. 3
Check the Response \-\- \fBrejected\fP means the central denied it
.IP 3. 3
Alternatively, find \fBLE Connection Update Complete\fP (HCI level)
.IP 4. 3
Check Status \-\- non\-zero means the controller rejected the update
.UNINDENT
.SS Vendor\-Specific Events
.sp
Vendor\-specific HCI events (event code 0xFF) contain
controller\-manufacturer diagnostic data. btmon decodes some vendor
events for known manufacturers (Intel, Broadcom, etc.) but many
sub\-events show as \fBUnknown\fP with raw hex data. These are expected
and generally not actionable without vendor documentation.
.sp
Intel controllers emit extended telemetry events (subevent 0x8780)
that include connection quality metrics, error counters, and firmware
state. Partial decoding is available in \fBmonitor/intel.c\fP\&.
